object member ordering is a regular C++ feature.

Even if it's nuanced, this is a common code so at least the initialization order will be common across sensor reactors.

> This won't extend to more complicated use cases like those in nvidia-gpu

Which use-case, can you please explain further?

This is which i found in nvidia code

```
int main()
{
    boost::asio::io_context io;
    auto systemBus = std::make_shared<sdbusplus::asio::connection>(io);
    sdbusplus::asio::object_server objectServer(systemBus, true);
    objectServer.add_manager("/xyz/openbmc_project/sensors");
    objectServer.add_manager("/xyz/openbmc_project/inventory");
    systemBus->request_name("xyz.openbmc_project.GpuSensor");
    mctp::MctpRequester mctpRequester(io);
```

so if we store the `io_context` in a class instance, where is the difference?

You can still form areference to it and such, just as before.
